Helium services is the self-hosted side of the Helium browser: extension
downloads, component updates, uBlock Origin filter lists, the bangs list,
and spellcheck dictionaries.

The package starts the local programs. It does not publish them. Finish
the steps below before pointing a browser at this machine.

What is not included
--------------------
components (the separate Omaha, crlset, and translation server), minipush,
minidumpster, sparkler, and release-collector are other upstream programs.
Browser self-update, web push, and crash reports are not served here.
Docker, a private nginx, and acme.sh are not used. TLS is yours: put a
certificate on the vhost, or terminate TLS on another proxy in front of it.

1. Pick the web server
----------------------
Installing helium-services also installs the nginx integration. Install
helium-services-apache as well when this machine uses Apache. Enable one
vhost, not both.

2. Name the host
----------------
Edit /etc/helium-services/helium-services.env. Set SERVICES_HOSTNAME,
PROXY_BASE_URL, and UBO_PROXY_BASE_URL to the same host. PROXY_BASE_URL
ends in /ext/ and UBO_PROXY_BASE_URL ends in /ubo/. An empty HMAC_SECRET
is filled in on first install. Leave LISTEN and UBO_LISTEN on 127.0.0.1
unless you also edit the vhost.

Restart the backends after saving the file:

  systemctl restart helium-services-extension-proxy helium-services-ubo

3. Nginx
--------
Edit server_name in /etc/nginx/sites-available/helium-services.conf, then:

  ln -s ../sites-available/helium-services.conf \
      /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/helium-services.conf
  nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx

The default site is left alone. This file answers only its server_name.

4. Apache
---------
Edit ServerName in /etc/httpd/conf/webapps.available/helium-services.conf.
If this is the only VirtualHost on port 80, Apache treats it as the
default site, so add another default vhost first when this machine serves
other names. Then:

  ln -s ../webapps.available/helium-services.conf \
      /etc/httpd/conf/webapps.d/helium-services.conf
  apachectl configtest && systemctl reload httpd

5. Point Helium at it
---------------------
In Helium, set helium.services.origin_override to the origin of the vhost
you enabled, with no path. Example: https://services.example.com

The master switch is helium.services.enabled. These switches select which
paths that origin is used for:

  helium.services.ext_proxy
  helium.services.bangs
  helium.services.spellcheck_files
  helium.services.ublock_assets

The browser then requests /ext/, /com, /bangs.json, /dict/, and /ubo/
on that origin.

6. Dictionaries and bangs
-------------------------
/bangs.json is shipped with the package. helium-services-bangs.timer
replaces it from BANG_SOURCE about two minutes after boot, then hourly.
/dict/ is empty until helium-services-dicts.timer has downloaded the
pinned Chromium hunspell snapshot. That run needs outbound HTTPS. It
starts on the same delay, then repeats daily. Spellcheck downloads fail
until that first run succeeds.

7. uBlock Origin assets
-----------------------
This snapshot still requests the uBlock Origin 1.76.4 assets file.
Helium 0.22.3 ships uBlock Origin 1.77.0. To point the proxy at another
assets.json, set both UBO_ASSETS_JSON_URL and UBO_ASSETS_JSON_SHA256.
Do not set those together with UBO_USE_ORIGINAL_UBLOCK_ASSETS. Clients
must send Accept-Encoding: br. A browser check:

  curl -D- -o /dev/null -H 'Accept-Encoding: br' \
      http://127.0.0.1:8092/assets.json

The extension proxy answers:

  curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8091/health

Logs are in the journal:

  journalctl -u helium-services-extension-proxy -u helium-services-ubo \
      -u helium-services-bangs -u helium-services-dicts
